    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Sten Esaias Tegnér was a Swedish businessman whose working life extended across the Atlantic. He owned and operated an American electronics company and a hotel, having spent a decade in the United States before moving to London. His public roles included chairing the Friends of the Nordic Museum and Skansen.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "During the pandemic, Sten Tegnér argued that Stockholm's Nordic Museum and Skansen needed exceptional help. As chairman of their friends' association, he announced a grant divided equally between the institutions. The board allowed their directors to decide how to use it, departing from its usual support for specified projects. The Nordic Museum planned improvements to Julita Manor as a visitor destination, while Skansen needed help through closure.\n\nHis charitable giving also included a donation with his wife, Ingeborg, to the Swedish Child Diabetes Foundation. The foundation acknowledged their joint contribution among the gifts received during the final two months of 2011.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 85,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Museum’s original press release documents the collective grant and intended uses. Recipient foundation’s full indexed donor table independently records the named couple’s contribution."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 101,
    "html": "<p>During the pandemic, Sten Tegnér argued that Stockholm's Nordic Museum and Skansen needed exceptional help. As chairman of their friends' association, he announced a grant divided equally between the institutions. The board allowed their directors to decide how to use it, departing from its usual support for specified projects. The Nordic Museum planned improvements to Julita Manor as a visitor destination, while Skansen needed help through closure.</p>\r\n<p>His charitable giving also included a donation with his wife, Ingeborg, to the Swedish Child Diabetes Foundation. The foundation acknowledged their joint contribution among the gifts received during the final two months of 2011.</p>\r\n"
  },
